1. Kotlin is a Compiled Language
Kotlin doesn't run directly on your computer. Instead, it goes through several steps:

text
Your Kotlin Code (.kt) 
    ↓
Kotlin Compiler 
    ↓
Java Bytecode (.class files)
    ↓
Java Virtual Machine (JVM)
    ↓
Your Computer
Visual Example:
kotlin
// Your Kotlin code
fun main() {
    println("Hello World")
}

// Gets compiled to Java bytecode (simplified)
// public static void main(String[] args) {
//     System.out.println("Hello World");
// }

// Then the JVM runs it
2. The Compilation Process
Step-by-step:
kotlin
// Step 1: You write Kotlin code
val name = "Nova"
println(name)

// Step 2: Kotlin compiler (kotlinc) checks for errors
// - Are types correct?
// - Are all variables defined?
// - Is the syntax valid?

// Step 3: Compiler converts to Java bytecode
// - .kt files become .class files
// - This is what the JVM understands

// Step 4: JVM executes the bytecode
// - Loads the class
// - Runs main() function
// - Outputs: Nova
3. Key Concepts of How Kotlin Works
A. Everything is an Object
kotlin
// Even primitive types are objects
val number = 42          // Int object
val text = "Hello"       // String object
val list = listOf(1,2,3) // List object

// You can call methods on ANYTHING
println(number.toString())  // "42"
println(text.length)        // 5
println(list.size)          // 3
B. Type Inference
kotlin
// Kotlin figures out types automatically
val name = "Nova"     // Inferred as String
val age = 25          // Inferred as Int
val price = 19.99     // Inferred as Double

// But you can also specify types explicitly
val name: String = "Nova"
val age: Int = 25
C. Null Safety
kotlin
// By default, variables CANNOT be null
var name: String = "Nova"
name = null  // ❌ ERROR! Null can not be a value of String

// You must explicitly allow null
var name: String? = "Nova"
name = null  // ✅ This is allowed

// You must handle null before using
println(name?.length)  // Safe call - returns null if name is null
println(name!!.length) // Force - throws error if null (use carefully!)
D. Compile-Time vs Runtime
kotlin
// COMPILE-TIME (checked when you build)
val x = 5
val y = "hello"
// x = y  // ❌ ERROR: Type mismatch - caught at compile time

// RUNTIME (checked when program runs)
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3)
// numbers[5]  // ❌ ERROR: IndexOutOfBounds - happens at runtime
4. Memory Management
Stack vs Heap:
kotlin
fun example() {
    // Stack memory (fast, automatic)
    val localVar = 42      // Stored on stack
    val localString = "Hi" // Reference on stack, string on heap
    
    // Heap memory (slower, managed by garbage collector)
    val bigObject = MyClass() // Stored on heap
}

// Garbage Collection - Kotlin automatically cleans up unused objects
fun createTemp() {
    val temp = "I'm temporary"
    // When this function ends, 'temp' is eligible for garbage collection
}
