The Dot Operator (.) Explained
The dot operator is used to access members of an object. It's like saying "give me this property or function from that object."

1. Accessing Properties
kotlin
val person = Person("Alice", 25)

// Dot operator accesses properties
val name = person.name    // Get the name property
val age = person.age      // Get the age property

// It works like: "person's name", "person's age"
2. Calling Functions/Methods
kotlin
val text = "Hello"

// Dot operator calls functions
val length = text.length      // Call length function
val upper = text.uppercase()  // Call uppercase() function
val chars = text.toCharArray() // Call toCharArray() function

// It means: "text's length", "text's uppercase()"
3. Chaining with Dots
kotlin
val text = "  Hello World  "

// Multiple dots chain operations
val result = text
    .trim()          // Remove spaces
    .uppercase()     // Convert to uppercase
    .substring(0, 5) // Get first 5 chars

// Each dot accesses the result of the previous operation
// Result: "HELL"
4. Accessing Nested Objects
kotlin
val user = User(
    name = "Nova",
    address = Address(
        street = "123 Main St",
        city = "New York"
    )
)

// Multiple dots for nested access
val city = user.address.city
// Means: "user's address's city"
5. Static Access (Companion Objects)
kotlin
class MyClass {
    companion object {
        const val VERSION = "1.0"
        fun create(): MyClass = MyClass()
    }
}

// Dot accesses companion members
val version = MyClass.VERSION   // Access static property
val instance = MyClass.create() // Call static function
6. Extension Functions (Custom Dot Operations)
kotlin
// Add new function using dot operator
fun String.isEmail(): Boolean = this.contains("@")

val email = "test@email.com"
val isValid = email.isEmail()  // Custom dot operation
// Means: "is email a valid email?"
7. Lambda/Anonymous Functions
kotlin
val numbers = listOf(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)

// Dot with lambda
val doubled = numbers.map { it * 2 }
// Means: "numbers mapped using this function"

// Multiple dots with lambdas
val result = numbers
    .filter { it % 2 == 0 }  // Keep even numbers
    .map { it * it }         // Square them
    .sum()                   // Add them up
// Result: 20 (2² + 4² = 4 + 16 = 20)
8. Dot vs No Dot
kotlin
// With dot: accessing object's members
val text = "Hello"
val length = text.length  // Dot means "text's length"

// Without dot: functions in scope
fun printText() {
    println("Hi")  // No dot - function in current scope
}

val max = maxOf(10, 20)  // No dot - top-level function
9. Scoping with Dots
kotlin
// The dot creates a scope chain
class Car {
    var brand = "Tesla"
    var model = "Model 3"
    
    fun start() {
        // Inside, you can use dot to access
        val info = "${this.brand} ${this.model}"  // this.brand = this car's brand
        println(info)
    }
}

val myCar = Car()
myCar.start()  // Dot accesses the Car object
