Final Example: Putting It All Together
kotlin
fun main() {
    // Compile-time: Kotlin checks types
    // Runtime: Program executes
    // Compiled: Becomes bytecode
    
    val users = listOf(
        User("Alice", 25),
        User("Bob", 30),
        User("Charlie", 35)
    )
    
    // Multiple dots = multiple accesses
    val result = users           // List<User>
        .filter { it.age > 28 } // List<User> (filtered)
        .map { it.name }        // List<String> (names only)
        .sorted()               // List<String> (sorted)
        .joinToString(", ")     // String (joined)
    
    // Each dot says: "take the result and access this function"
    println(result)  // Output: Bob, Charlie
}
Does this deeper explanation help? The dot operator is essentially Kotlin's way of saying "of" or "belongs to" - it connects objects to their members!









